"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 18 and an ISUP grade group 3 Gleason pattern from the prostatectomy specimen. Positive findings include negative surgical margins and no documented seminal vesicle invasion or lymphovascular invasion. A significant limitation is that no lymph nodes were removed, meaning nodal status cannot be assessed, which widens uncertainty regarding risk stratification. The biopsy-to-prostatectomy comparison is limited as the pathology report notes missing Gleason pattern and ISUP grade from the initial biopsy session.\n\nThe trajectory of postoperative PSA remains unknown, and this information would significantly revise prognosis. Future follow-up data are also needed to better characterize recurrence risk. Given the current data, the prognostic assessment relies heavily on the pathological findings in the prostatectomy specimen while acknowledging the missing nodal sampling. The fixed estimated horizon is 62.79 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 40.81–84.77 months; its coverage has not been validated."
